From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 14:36:41 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] kk: emulate sampler min/max only for draws with a reduction
 sampler bound

The min/max reduction emulation before Apple10 cost every sample, also
through ordinary samplers: the per-sample branch on the descriptor's
filter flags (and the non-inlined footprint call) doubled the GPU time of
sampling-bound shaders (fragment texture() 7.5 -> 14.8 ms, compute
textureLod 1.9 -> 4.0 ms on an M1 Max) and made their compiles 1.3-1.6x
slower, even in applications that never create a reduction sampler.

Pipelines whose samplers come from descriptor sets now have two programs:
one that emulates min/max only for immutable samplers (resolved when the
pipeline is compiled: immutable samplers that all reduce are emulated
without a branch, others are left alone; their filter flags are part of
the set layout hash), and a minmax program that also emulates the samplers
of the sets it records in kk_shader_info::minmax_sets. Descriptor sets
count the reduction samplers written or copied into them (never
decremented; a copy of mutable descriptors counts one if the source set
has any) and push descriptor sets those pushed in the command buffer.
Draws and dispatches use the minmax program only while one of those sets
holds a reduction sampler.

The program a pipeline compiles at creation is the one without emulation,
unless a set of one of those layouts already got a reduction sampler (a
descriptor heap like vkd3d-proton's sampler heap is then bound with every
draw), or, for pipelines read from a pipeline cache, any set; the other
program is compiled on first use (MESA_KK_DEBUG=minmax logs reduction
samplers, programs compiled at creation and on first use, and replays).
Both programs' MSL is built at creation (pipeline caches serialize both);
the minmax program's while Metal compiles the other one on another thread.

Update-after-bind sets may get a reduction sampler after recording: draws
that used the program without emulation record such sets, and a command
buffer submitted after one of them got a reduction sampler is re-recorded
from its command queue (the replay decides on the final counts).
One-time-submit command buffers can't be replayed and use the minmax
program for update-after-bind sets right away.

M1 Max, 403 pipelines (gamescope, FSR2, dxil-spirv references), Metal
source cache defeated, median of 3 runs: 59.96 s vs 59.89 s with the
emulation compiled out (1.001; sampling shaders 1.002-1.006); with a
reduction sampler in every layout 69.78 s (1.165, as before). Runtime with
ordinary samplers equals the emulation compiled out (fragment 7.46-7.49 ms
both, compute 1.85/2.41 ms both); through a MIN sampler unchanged.
